Define an ordinary Macro in your data source, query it by name, and let Basics render the positional children. This example deliberately keeps the Macro outside library defaults:

```tsx
import { MacroDataDriver, SnlSyntaxTreeView, parseSnlSyntaxTree } from '@sjtu-ai4math/snl-basics'
import type { SnlMacro } from '@sjtu-ai4math/snl-basics'
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css'
import '@sjtu-ai4math/snl-basics/style.css'

const equals: SnlMacro = {
  name: 'equals', description: 'Equality', kind: 'const',
  source: { entries: [], urls: [] }, tags: [], dynamic_arity: false,
  styles: [{ style_name: 'default', tags: [],
    template: { mode: 'formula_inline', body: '#0 = #1' } }],
}
const macros = new MacroDataDriver({
  queries: { query_macro: async ({ macro_name }) => macro_name === 'equals' ? equals : null },
})
const tree = parseSnlSyntaxTree('equals($x$, $x$)')
export function Example() {
  return <SnlSyntaxTreeView tree={tree} macro_data_driver={macros} />
}
```

The formula leaves intentionally use literal delimiters. Plain prose belongs in `%…%`; raw KaTeX commands outside formula delimiters are not an alternative SNL grammar. For binders and source references, use the semantic-resolution/binding APIs and test the resulting target paths, not only this example's printed formula.

Use the non-throwing parser when editing incomplete user input. Keep the original source and display diagnostics; do not silently replace a failed parse with a plausible formula.
